  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a swing bridge work?",
      "a": "The whole span sits on a pier in the river and rotates horizontally, usually through about 90 degrees, to line up with the river so ships can pass on either side.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a swing bridge?",
        "How does a turning bridge open?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why are swing bridge arms equal on both sides?",
      "a": "Equal arms balance each other around the pivot, so the span does not tip while it turns, and the motor only has to overcome friction rather than lift anything.",
      "asks": [
        "Why doesn't a swing bridge tip over?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What are the rollers for?",
      "a": "A ring of rollers running on a circular track under the span helps carry its weight and keeps it level as it turns.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a swing bridge turn on?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What turns a swing bridge?",
      "a": "Usually an electric or hydraulic motor drives a small pinion gear that meshes with a large ring gear around the pier, so the span rotates slowly and smoothly.",
      "asks": [
        "What powers a swing bridge?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What happens when a swing bridge closes?",
      "a": "Once it lines up with the road, its ends are lifted or wedged onto fixed supports and locked, so traffic loads go into the abutments and not only the central pivot.",
      "asks": [
        "How is a swing bridge locked?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A swing bridge usually rotates through about 90 degrees on a central pier, leaving a navigation channel on one or both sides.",
    "The El Ferdan Railway Bridge across the Suez Canal in Egypt is one of the longest swing bridges in the world, about 340 metres long."
  ],
